**SESSION 2 (2026-07-22) — root-caused the paged-prefill residual to CUTLASS mainloop codegen quality (MEASURED, not hand-waved).** Approaches tried + results (per-tile+LPT baseline = s2k 0.838 / s16k 0.874 / qwen 0.889):
- **TMA_Q decoupled from Use_TMA_KV** (FA3's Use_TMA_Q!=Use_TMA_KV: Q never paged -> TMA Q reload even when K/V uses COOP cp.async). Added `bool TMA_Q=USE_TMA` param to prefill_persist_kernel, tmaq_gqa reuses the validated dense-GQA 3D Q TMA box. Paged persist WITH TMA_Q still 0.78-0.82 (K/V COOP gather latency the single-CTA ring can't hide). Infra kept, branch DISABLED.
- **K-ahead-of-V (separate fullK/fullV barriers, FA3 IntraWGOverlap load)**: consumer waits fullK[st] for QK, fullV[pst] for PV; producer loads all K (publish fullK) then all V (publish fullV). REGRESSED per-tile (s16k 0.874->0.851, qwen 0.889->0.872): with STAGES=3 the producer already stays far enough ahead that V[i] is ready, so the split only added doubled __shfl passes + extra mbarrier round-trips. REVERTED. (Would only help at STAGES=2 like FA3.)
- **Paged persist PSTAGES=2** (FA3's exact kStages=2, 160KB): 0.62-0.64 — my hand-written cp.async ring STARVES at 2 stages; PSTAGES=3 (0.80) hides the gather better but still loses to per-tile. cutlass hides the same gather with 2 stages.
- **ncu SMOKING GUN (s16k, minimal wave-quant/tail)**: FA3 device_kernel (persist, 132 CTAs) = **74.83% tensor pipe util**; my per-tile prefill_kernel (4096 CTAs) = **60.91%** on the ALGORITHMICALLY IDENTICAL mainloop (same 128x128x128 tiles, RS-PV, QK(i)/PV(i-1) 2-group IntraWGOverlap, hardware ex2.approx, ping-pong). s2k: FA3 57.5% vs mine 47.8%. FA3 STALLS MORE (wait 1.99 vs 1.65, long_scoreboard 1.46 vs 0.39) yet computes more -> its warps are tensor-bound, mine are overhead-bound (issuing filler: addr-gen/pack/loop). The tensor-util ratio (0.81-0.83) ~= the perf ratio. This is SASS instruction-scheduling quality (ptxas/cutlass), and it manifests REGARDLESS of persist vs per-tile (persist can't beat it; even a perfect persist tops out at the per-tile mainloop efficiency). The causal-triangle light-CTA penalty compounds it for short-ctx.
- Consumer QK/PV ARE separate wgmma groups (qk_batch_g commits, pv_boxes commits) -> wgmma_wait1 correctly gates QK; schedule is correct. "issue QK(i+1) before rescale(i)" == old qk_rs_pv_g which was WORSE (delays PV, kills QK||PV overlap) -> not the lever.

**COMMITTED (af89897, branch bench/sol-execbench-real-shapes, 2026-07-22):** the banked speedups (per-tile+LPT, paged persist DISABLED). 105/105 correct, geomean ~1.14x. Also updated bench/plot.py numbers + docs/per_scenario_speedup.png. Experimental persist-prefetch snapshot saved in `.experimental_stash/` (gitignored).

**KEY INSIGHT (no codegen ceiling): my dense-causal hd128 per-tile mainloop BEATS FA3 (1.01-1.05x)** using the SAME schedule FA3 uses (verified via the FA3 mainloop extraction + my SASS: QK(i)+PV(i+1) issued together, WarpScheduler NamedBarrier ping-pong between the 2 MMA WGs, wgmma_wait1 leaves PV in flight during softmax's exp2, rescale_o after PV, deferred row-sum/norm; RS-PV, hardware ex2.approx, consumer regs 232 via setmaxnreg). So the paged-prefill residual is NOT the mainloop -- it is the 128-thread cp.async COOP producer's per-light-causal-m-tile pipeline-fill prologue (dense's TMA prologue is ~free -> dense wins; paged's COOP prologue is paid per light m-tile of the causal triangle -> paged loses). FA3 amortizes it with the persistent kernel.

**Prefetched-scheduler persist (session 2, FAILED):** hid the contended atomicAdd behind the loads (FA3 prefetch_next_work). REGRESSED paged (0.72) AND the dense persist it shares (causal 1.05->1.00, gqa 1.01->0.96) -- for dense the TMA producer has no loads to hide the atomic behind, so the restructure only added overhead. The persist's measured bottleneck is barrier stall (ncu 1.46 vs FA3 0.44) = producer falling behind the continuous ring so the consumer stalls on full[]. Stashed. NEXT lever to try: make the COOP producer LIGHTER (less issue-slot contention with the consumers) or a dedicated lean paged persist; the shared dense/paged persist kernel forces compromises.

**SESSION 3 (2026-07-22) — exhaustively tested the FA3-exact persist recipe; ALL persist variants lose to per-tile. Persist is a confirmed dead-end for this hand-written kernel.** A parallel Workflow reverse-engineered FA3's exact paged persist config (bf16 hd128 causal, page_size=1): kBlockM=kBlockN=128, **kStages=2**, **Q SINGLE-buffered** (FA3 has ~67KB headroom but does NOT double-buffer; the work-tile boundary serializes on Q in FA3 too -> QBUF is NOT the lever), K and V on **SEPARATE PipelineAsync<2> with K loaded one n_block AHEAD of V** (IntraWGOverlap at the load level), 128-thread cp.async producer. FA3 compiles 3 distinct kernels (dense-TMA / paged-TMA / paged-cp.async) at compile time; my single shared dense+paged persist kernel is the compromise it avoids. Measured persist variants (all vs per-tile 0.84-0.88):
- STAGES=2, QBUF=1, fused K/V barrier: 0.62 (starves)
- STAGES=2, QBUF=2, fullK/fullV split (K-ahead): 0.68 (K-ahead helped +0.06 but 2 stages still starve)
- STAGES=3, QBUF=1, fused: 0.80 (best persist; still < per-tile)
- STAGES=3, QBUF=1, fullK/fullV split (K-ahead): 0.77 (K-ahead HURTS at STAGES=3 -- producer already far enough ahead, split just adds fullV-wait overhead)
- prefetched atomic scheduler: 0.72
LESSON: the synthesis was CONFIDENT K-ahead would un-starve STAGES=2 to parity; the measurement said 0.68. Stages dominate; my hand-written cp.async ring needs 3 stages where cutlass's 2 suffice, and even STAGES=3 (0.80) loses to per-tile (0.87) because the HARDWARE block scheduler (per-tile, 4096 CTAs) transitions work faster than my software persist machinery (scheduler handshake + continuous ring + Q handshake). The templated-QBUF + fullK/fullV split code is in `.experimental_stash/fa3_hopper_kahead_persist.cu` (gitignored). Reverted to committed af89897. **per-tile+LPT (0.84-0.89) remains the paged-prefill ceiling for this implementation.**

**Occupancy 2-CTA/SM (session 3, INFEASIBLE):** tried paged per-tile at CN=64/STAGES=2/MINB=2 (~96KB -> 2 CTAs fit smem) for cross-CTA latency hiding (the one lever the 1-CTA/SM persist can't use). HUNG. Register math: warp-specialized realloc = producer 40 + consumer 128/232 regs = ~38K regs/CTA; 2 CTAs = ~76K > the 64K SM register file. Warp specialization trades occupancy for register-rich consumers -> 2 CTAs/SM is IMPOSSIBLE for this design (FA3 also runs 1 CTA/SM). Reverted. So the paged residual cannot be closed by occupancy either. **Confirmed ceiling for this cutlass-free warp-specialized kernel: per-tile+LPT 0.84-0.89 on paged prefill. Levers exhausted: mainloop (beats FA3), persist (5 variants lose), occupancy (register-infeasible). The last ~13% is CUTLASS persist/pipeline codegen quality.**

**SESSION 4 (2026-07-22) — banked a faithful win (COMMITTED fe7ee46); ruled out K-ahead again.**
- **WIN (committed fe7ee46): dropped the redundant per-tile `fence.proxy.async` in the COOP (paged) consumer.** The `cp.async.mbarrier.arrive -> full[]` completion already orders the gather before the wgmma (same async proxy) -- FA3/CUTLASS's cp.async consumer has NO steady-state fence (verified in the mainloop extraction). Removing it: paged prefill s2k 0.840->0.854, s16k 0.861->0.875, qwen 0.887->0.906; full suite 105/105, geomean 1.146->1.158x, now **0 configs <0.85**. Faithful, correct, no regressions. This is the pattern that works: find + remove overhead FA3 doesn't have.
- **ncu paged-s16k stall comparison (mine vs FA3 device_kernel):** tensor 61.3% vs 74.8%; barrier 0.73 vs **0.44**; long_scoreboard 0.37 vs 1.14; wait 1.66 vs 1.99; dispatch_stall 1.09 vs 1.12 (SAME -> inherent softmax SFU, not the lever); warps_eligible 0.80 vs 0.69. FA3 stalls MORE on loads+wgmma (productive, tensor-pipe saturated) and LESS on barriers. My residual = the +0.29 barrier stall.
- **K-ahead (separate fullK/fullV) RE-TESTED with shfl-once producer (no doubled __shfl): REGRESSED HARD** -- paged 0.854->0.710 AND decode 1.06->0.986 (decode's CM=64 path shares prefill_kernel). So the barrier stall is NOT the shared-full[] K+V wait (K-ahead would help); splitting only ADDS barrier round-trips + a worse cp.async issue pattern (fused interleaved K/V issue beats split). The shared full[] is BETTER for this hand-written kernel. The residual barrier stall is the PING-PONG (nb_sync/nb_arrive, same structure as FA3's WarpScheduler NamedBarrier but my WGs desync more) -- not cleanly reducible without losing the WG-overlap that FA3 also relies on.
- **De-scatter (gather paged KV -> contiguous -> fast dense TMA prefill) would close it (~0.95-1.05x) but is NOT faithful** (real serving avoids it: paged KV exists for prefix-sharing/no-fragmentation; FA3 gathers inline). User/hook rejected it.

**SESSION 4 SASS DIFF (the concrete lever): FA3's paged-prefill PV uses `HGMMA.64x128x16 .tnspB` (8/iter, n128); MINE uses 2x `HGMMA.64x64x16` (16/iter, n64 boxes).** So my steady-state consumer issues 24 HGMMA/iter vs FA3's 16 -- 8 extra wgmma + fences per iter, diluting the tensor pipe (61% vs 75%) and competing with softmax for issue slots. Everything else matches (QK is m64n128k16 both; EX2 66 both; pack 32 both; FP32 ALU ~266-282 both; consumer_wait: cutlass does test_wait-then-wait, no fence -- I match after fence removal). THE FIX: switch bf16 PV (pv_boxes/pv_box64_body) from 2x m64n64k16 to 1x m64n128k16 (transB=1 already used). Blocker: my voffR V layout has dv in 2 64-col blocks (BOX=CN*128 apart); a single m64n128 needs dv contiguous over 128 (or the descriptor SBO to span the blocks) -- resolving the exact descriptor/layout (may need a producer-side V relayout so dv is contiguous). QK is unaffected (its n128 reads the key dim which IS contiguous in koffR). This is the first concrete faithful FA3-matching lever found via SASS; pursuing it.

**SESSION 5 (2026-07-22) -- SASS diff found 2 more faithful levers; 1 win, 1 regress.**
- **WIN (committed c67b976): PV as ONE m64n128k16 (transB) instead of 2x m64n64k16.** FA3's paged PV is 8x HGMMA.64x128x16.tnspB; mine was 16x m64n64 -> 8 extra wgmma+fences/iter. My voffR V smem is ALREADY a canonical GMMA MN-major layout (verified vs cute make_gmma_desc<Major::MN>): descriptor LBO == dv-block stride == CN*128 == +BOX places dv[64,128) where box-1 read it, so 1 m64n128 covers all 128 dv, acc reg->(dv,row) mapping UNCHANGED (rescale_o_g + O-writeback untouched). Added pv_box128_body; pv_boxes emits HDV/128 n128 boxes + trailing n64 for HDV%128. 105/105, geomean 1.158->1.166x, paged s2k 0.854->0.859 / s16k ~0.872 / qwen 0.906->0.912. Also lifts decode/dense sharing pv_boxes.
